  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1214 AM CDT Tue Aug 4 2026\n\nRain/storm chances... Latest surface obs show a cold front \nassociated with a broken line of showers/storms draped over \neastern Minnesota as of early this morning. Front will continue to\nimpinge on northwest Wisconsin over the next couple of hours, \nthough shower activity should become more scattered upon reaching \nthe forecast area mid-morning. Robust capping and remnant dry air\nto around 800 mb from departing high pressure should keep showers\nmostly isolated through early this afternoon, before CAMs show \nre-development occurring along the front during peak heating as \nthe cap erodes. Though the severe threat is low, skinny CAPE \nprofiles (1,000 to 1,200 J/kg) and near inverted-V profiles would \nsuggest gusty winds as a potential hazard with any storms as \ndaytime mixing taps into 25 to 30 knot winds at the top of the dry\nlayer. \n\nConvergence along the front ramps up across southern Wisconsin \nearly this evening as PWATs in proximity to the surface low \nincrease to near 2\". As such, most recent suite of model runs \nretains the axis of heaviest precip off to our south, with NBM\nprobabilistic guidance only showing a 20 to 40% signal for\nreceiving 1\" of rainfall localized to east-central Wisconsin.\nLight shower activity may continue into Wednesday as the low\ntracks over southern Michigan and cyclonic flow lingers, though\nmost areas should remain dry.\n\nLate week rain/storm chances... As the synoptic pattern de-\namplifies and zonal flow establishes itself across the CONUS mid-\nweek, expect a brief dry period through Thursday. Flattened upper\ntrough and attendant surface low then approach the Midwest on\nFriday, bringing our next widespread shower/storm chances that\nlast through the weekend. Predictability in storm coverage and\nintensity remains low this far out, as these will likely hinge on\nhow western ridge evolves throughout the week and how much\nassociated heat/humidity we can get into our neck of the woods.\nCursory glance at ML guidance paints a broad swath of 5 to 15%\nprobs over the upper Mississippi Valley for Days 5 and 6, though \nthis will continue to be monitored in the coming days.\n\nSmoke... As post-FROPA winds shift to the west later this \nmorning, shower activity along the front may help mix elevated\nsmoke plume down to the surface at times through this evening. No\nsignificant reductions in visibility are expected, though air \nquality may degrade to moderate (yellow category).",
